 In this case, the lock file, if existing, will become read-only, no write operation will be performed on it.
 However, dependency resolution step will still be performed if needed.
 
+## Merge lock files
+
+When several people work on the same project, `pdm.lock` is a frequent source of version control
+conflicts: two branches add or upgrade different packages and the merged file no longer reflects
+either resolution. `pdm merge-lock` reconciles two diverged revisions of a lock file against their
+common ancestor, without contacting any package index:
+
+```bash
+pdm merge-lock BASE OURS THEIRS [-o OUTPUT] [--on-conflict STRATEGY]
+```
+
+The three positional arguments are the paths of the lock file at the merge base, at "our" revision
+(the working copy) and at "their" revision (the incoming change). The merged lock file is written
+to `--output`, or overwrites `OURS` when the option is omitted, so the command can be wired as a
+[git merge driver](https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes#_defining_a_custom_merge_driver):
+
+```bash
+# .git/config or ~/.gitconfig
+[merge "pdm-lock"]
+    name = PDM lockfile merge driver
+    driver = pdm merge-lock %O %A %B
+```
+
+```gitattributes
+# .gitattributes
+pdm.lock merge=pdm-lock
+```
+
+Packages are matched across the three revisions by requirement identity (name, extras, marker and
+source URL) -- the pinned version itself is *not* part of the identity, so a version bump on one
+branch is merged with an unrelated change on the other. The merge follows the usual three-way
+rules:
+
+- a package added or removed on only one side, or changed identically on both sides, is merged
+  cleanly;
+- when both sides changed the same package, fields are merged individually: `groups`,
+  `dependencies` and file hashes are combined, and single-valued fields such as `version`,
+  `requires_python`, `summary` and `marker` must have at most one side changing them;
+- anything else -- e.g. both sides pinning different versions, or one side removing a package the
+  other side upgraded -- is a conflict.
+
+With the default `--on-conflict=fail`, conflicts are reported on stderr and the command exits with
+a non-zero code without writing anything. The other strategies resolve every conflict
+automatically: `--on-conflict=ours` and `--on-conflict=theirs` keep the entry from the given side,
+and `--on-conflict=newer` keeps the higher pinned version (a modification always wins over a
+removal, and ties are resolved in favor of `theirs`).
+
+Two more options help automation around the merge:
+
+- `--dry-run` only checks whether the lock files can be merged: it prints the summary, writes
+  nothing, and exits non-zero when conflicts remain. It is useful in CI to detect lock file
+  conflicts early.
+- `--json` prints a machine readable report to stdout, with the merge outcome, the list of added,
+  removed and updated packages, and the details of every conflict (unresolved or resolved by the
+  strategy). Human messages go to stderr in this mode so stdout stays parseable.
+
+```bash
+$ pdm merge-lock base.lock ours.lock theirs.lock --dry-run --json
+{
+  "changes": {
+    "added": ["requests 2.32.3"],
+    "removed": [],
+    "updated": ["urllib3 2.2.2"]
+  },
+  "clean": true,
+  "conflicts": [],
+  "packages": 42,
+  ...
+}
+```
+
+The `[metadata]` table is merged as well: `groups` and `targets` are combined, `lock_version` takes
+the highest side, and `content_hash` follows the working copy. The lock strategies
+(`cross_platform`, `static_urls`, ...) must not diverge in both directions -- in that case the
+lock files cannot be merged safely and `pdm merge-lock` refuses with an error; regenerate one of
+them with `pdm lock` so both use the same strategy.
+
+Only the PDM lock file format is supported; `pylock.toml` documents must be converted first, e.g.
+with `pdm lock --format pdm`.
+
 ## Lock strategies
 
 Currently, we support three flags to control the locking behavior: `cross_platform`, `static_urls` and `direct_minimal_versions`, with the meanings as follows.
